Roast.pet Weird Builder Projects =E2=80=94 4 useful new finds

Screened 25+ plausible candidates across pet cutout/matting, generated
sprites, editable media layouts, WebAR, captions, identity QA and commerce
tooling; prior-run finds were deduplicated. Four new projects are worth
retaining.
1) lightScout/light-converter =E2=80=94 REUSE NOW / FORK

URL: https://github.com/lightScout/light-converter
Creator: lightScout
Activity: crawled Sep 19, 2026; current project
License: MIT code; BiRefNet model MIT; IS-Net/DIS Apache-2.0

Roast.pet step solved: customer pet photo =E2=86=92 robust transparent cuto=
ut,
especially difficult low-contrast fur, with a built-in manual rescue path.

Why this is stronger than the generic browser background removers already
found: it automatically runs a second CLAHE contrast-enhanced pass when the
first mask is uncertain, then applies guided-filter edge refinement, hole
filling and speckle cleanup. It also has batch processing, restore/erase
brushes, IndexedDB persistence, model caching and ZIP export.

Install/demo route: clone, npm install, npm run models, npm run dev. WebGPU
uses fp16 BiRefNet; WASM fallback uses int8 IS-Net.
Dependencies/hardware: modern browser; WebGPU preferred, WASM fallback;
one-time model build uses Python/ONNX tooling.
Evidence: runnable browser implementation, explicit model
provenance/licenses, batch/edit workflow, uncertainty-triggered second pass=
.
Work replaced: most cutout worker UI, fallback logic, edge cleanup, caching
and human rescue tooling.
Integration: LOW-MEDIUM. Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM.
Exact experiment: run 100 real Etsy-style pet photos through Light
Converter and BG0: white dog/white wall, black cat/dark sofa, grass/fur,
whiskers, cropped ears, clutter. Record fully automatic pass rate, rescue
time, edge quality and weak-laptop latency. If the CLAHE fallback
materially reduces human intervention, fork its processing pipeline.
2) SeeleAI/CookSprite =E2=80=94 COPY WORKFLOW / BENCHMARK

URL: https://github.com/SeeleAI/CookSprite
Creator: SeeleAI
Activity: crawled Sep 19, 2026
License: verify repository/model licenses before embedding

Roast.pet step solved: generated static pet assets =E2=86=92 individually
regenerable frames through an agent-friendly typed workflow rather than
rerolling a whole sheet.

CookSprite separates model/inference, typed workflow and frontend/agent
CLI. Its useful Roast.pet idea is that frames are atomic operations:
generate, preview, pixel-fix and regenerate individual frames, then export
ordinary PNG sprite sheets. It also supports a GPU-free stub path and
ComfyUI export.

Install/API/demo route: repository provides inference HTTP boundary, typed
workflow graph, web toolbox and agent CLI.
Dependencies/hardware: backend-dependent; architecture permits local/docker
inference and a stub path.
Evidence: runnable repo with explicit ABI-decoupled layers and
individual-frame regeneration.
Work replaced: custom agent-to-image-model orchestration and much of the
regenerate-one-bad-asset workflow.
Integration: MEDIUM. Maintenance risk: MEDIUM.
Exact experiment: model Roast.pet=E2=80=99s approved pet package as 7 atomi=
c
operations: neutral, blink, side-eye, recoil, A/E/O mouths. Deliberately
fail one mouth and verify only that node reruns while approved outputs
remain immutable.
3) realZachi/shotluma =E2=80=94 COPY EDITABLE-DESIGN MECHANISM

URL: https://github.com/realZachi/shotluma
Creator: realZachi
Activity: crawled Sep 14, 2026
License: verify before code reuse

Roast.pet step solved: agent-created listing/social graphics that remain
editable instead of becoming opaque generated bitmaps.

Shotluma is a local-first browser canvas where the AI drives the same
normal editor operations a human uses. Text, gradients, shapes and mockups
remain real selectable layers; a single element can be revised without
regenerating the entire design.

Route: browser editor; projects local-first; agent can use local Codex/CLI
or provider key.
Dependencies/hardware: browser; no image-generation GPU required for layout
itself.
Evidence: current working editor with editable artboards and exact-size
export.
Work replaced: a custom agent graphics editor and the brittle pattern of
asking an image model to render typography/layout.
Integration: MEDIUM if reused; LOW if only copying its tool-call design.
Exact experiment: reproduce the canonical ROAST/Netflix-style card using
only editable operations: background, pet cutout, podium, headline, footer.
Then have an agent change only headline/pet image across 20 orders. Compare
failure rate against HTML templates.

Verdict: COPY EDITABLE-DESIGN MECHANISM. HTML remains the likely production
primitive, but this is a strong rescue/manual-editor pattern.
4) eigdoyr/mockymax =E2=80=94 COPY MASK ARCHITECTURE

URL: https://github.com/eigdoyr/mockymax/blob/main/README.md
Creator: eigdoyr
Status: v0.2.5 alpha; 14 scenes
License: verify before source reuse

Roast.pet step solved: one canonical product photograph =E2=86=92 reusable
compositing template with occlusions and perspective already baked into a
mask.

MockyMax represents each scene as a background plus alpha mask defining the
insertion surface. That naturally handles rounded corners, partial
visibility and foreground occlusion; its masks can be bootstrapped
automatically with SAM and hand-refined once.

Route: browser compositor.
Dependencies/hardware: ordinary browser for composition; SAM/Fal is only
needed to bootstrap masks.
Evidence: 14 shipped scenes and a working alpha-mask architecture.
Work replaced: repeated manual Photoshop masking for every product photo
and complicated per-order geometry.
Integration: LOW if we copy the artifact format.
Exact experiment: take five real Roast.pet product photos (card front, card
angle, framed poster, shirt, phone/AR), create one alpha/occlusion mask per
scene, then swap in 100 customer artworks. Compare against homography-only
placement on cards and shirts.

Verdict: COPY MASK ARCHITECTURE. The useful artifact is simply scene.jpg +
placement transform + mask.png.
TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES

   1. A pet-cutout rescue editor: Light Converter already combines
   BiRefNet, uncertainty fallback, guided edges and brushes.
   2. Whole-sheet regeneration logic: use atomic frame/row workflows like
   CookSprite/VexJoy.
   3. AI-rendered typography/layout: keep graphics as editable HTML/canvas
   operations.
   4. Per-order Photoshop product masking: make each product photograph a
   reusable background + transform + alpha/occlusion mask.
   5. Yet another generic background-removal model: benchmark the existing
   candidates and freeze one winner.

Most surprising hobby project

Light Converter. The automatic second CLAHE pass when a first segmentation
mask is uncertain is exactly the sort of tiny, practical mechanism that may
matter more for messy customer uploads than changing the segmentation model=
.
Fastest integration win

Clone Light Converter and run the same difficult 50=E2=80=93100 pet-photo b=
enchmark
against BG0. This can settle the cutout stack without touching the rest of
Roast.pet.
Repo/author worth following

lightScout/light-converter. It is focused on the unglamorous production
details=E2=80=94uncertainty fallback, edge refinement, batching, local pers=
istence
and manual rescue=E2=80=94that determine whether a customer-upload pipeline
actually works cheaply.
Architecture delta

The upload boundary should probably become:
customer photos =E2=86=92 local/batch BiRefNet =E2=86=92 uncertainty-trigge=
red contrast
retry =E2=86=92 deterministic edge cleanup =E2=86=92 optional brush rescue =
=E2=86=92 canonical
transparent pet =E2=86=92 immutable identity bank.

Generation should never be asked to solve background cleanup, and
downstream Roast.pet workers should never see the original messy upload
unless the cutout stage explicitly fails.

